
[38;5;231m⏺[39m Last clarifying question before I lay out approaches: [1mwhat actually produces log records.[0m

  The [38;5;153mconsole[39m calls in [38;5;153mapp.js[39m today only fire on paths someone already thought about — a validation failure, a login
  result. Production bugs are, almost by definition, the ones nobody anticipated: a TypeError in the submit handler, a
  promise that rejects with no [38;5;153m.catch[39m. Those currently produce nothing you'd ever see.

  - [1mGlobal error capture + explicit call sites[0m — install [38;5;153mwindow.onerror[39m and [38;5;153munhandledrejection[39m handlers in the browser,
    [38;5;153muncaughtException[39m/[38;5;153munhandledRejection[39m in Node, and convert the existing [38;5;153mconsole[39m calls to leveled logger calls. Buys
    the unanticipated crashes, which is the category you're actually missing. Costs some care: the handlers must never
    throw, and you need de-duplication so one error in a loop doesn't flood the endpoint.
  - [1mExplicit call sites only[0m — convert the existing [38;5;153mconsole[39m calls, add a few more. Cheap and predictable, but it only
    ever tells you about failures you already predicted.
  - [1mGlobal capture plus automatic network instrumentation[0m — the above, plus wrapping [38;5;153mfetch[39m to log request failures and
    slow calls. Buys visibility into the API layer, which is where a login form usually breaks. Costs a monkey-patch of
    a global, which complicates testing and can surprise future readers.

  [1mMy recommendation: global error capture plus explicit call sites.[0m The global handlers are the highest-value part of
  the whole change, and the [38;5;153mfetch[39m wrapper can be added later behind the same transport interface without reworking
  anything — whereas skipping global capture means the subsystem still misses the bugs you built it for. Note that
  [38;5;153mlogin()[39m is currently a stub that never makes a network call, so there's no real fetch traffic to instrument yet
  regardless.
